=== Iteration 1 ===
```
Instead of using nl.mean() directly which doesn't support reduction on partition axes, we'll compute the mean manually by summing the elements using nl.sum() (which operates on free axes) and dividing by the total number of elements, following the shape requirements for axis reduction.
```

=== Iteration 2 ===
```
Instead of trying to reduce on partition axes, we'll compute the mean by summing the elements using nl.sum() on all axes and dividing by the total number of elements, ensuring we initialize the result with the correct shape and buffer.
```

=== Iteration 3 ===
```
Changed nl.sum() call to include required axis parameter by specifying axis=(0,) since we want to reduce along all dimensions to get a scalar mean value, and ensured proper handling of the total number of elements for division
```

=== Iteration 4 ===
```
To compute mean, we'll iterate over batches of partition size 128 due to hardware limitations, accumulating sums and dividing by total elements at the end, since direct reduction on partition axes isn't supported
```

=== Iteration 5 ===
```
Initialize result with correct shape (a_tensor.shape) instead of empty tuple () to match input tensor dimensions, and compute mean using nl.mean directly since it's a built-in operation that handles reduction properly
```

=== Iteration 6 ===
```
Since the error shows a mismatch between NKI output (array) and PyTorch output (scalar), we need to compute the global mean by summing along all dimensions and dividing by total elements, using nl.load() to get input data and nl.sum() with axis=1 to reduce along all non-partition dimensions
```

=== Iteration 7 ===
```
To fix the shape mismatch error, we need to use nl.mean with axis=tuple(range(len(a_tensor.shape))) to compute the mean across all dimensions and ensure the result shape matches the initialized empty scalar tensor.
```

=== Iteration 8 ===
```
Since reduction on partition axes is not supported and we need to compute a global mean, we'll process the input in chunks using nl.affine_range to iterate over blocks of size nl.tile_size.pmax, accumulating the sum and dividing by total elements at the end.
```

=== Iteration 9 ===
```
Initialize acc with a 1D shape (1,) instead of empty shape () to avoid tuple index out of range error, while maintaining scalar output functionality
```

=== Iteration 10 ===
```
To fix the 'Insufficient rank!' error when using nl.zeros(), we need to ensure the accumulator's shape has sufficient dimensions to match the input tensor by using nl.zeros((1, 1), dtype=nl.float32, buffer=nl.psum) instead of a single dimension.
```

